---
import { getCollection } from "astro:content";

const projects = await getCollection("projects");
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
	<head>
		<meta charset="UTF-8" />
		<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
		<title>Noodlestan</title>
	</head>
	<body>
		<header>
			<h1>Noodlestan</h1>
		</header>

		<main>
			<section id="hero">
				<h2>Writing software because. Just because.</h2>
				<p>
					A home for software projects, experiments, ideas, and things
					worth building.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section id="projects">
				<h2>Projects</h2>
				<p>
					Tools for working with code and metadata, component libraries
					and design systems, AI agent tooling, documentation
					infrastructure, and creative experiments.
				</p>
				<p>
					The projects are independent, but many are connected and built
					to work together.
				</p>

				{projects.map((project) => (
					<article>
						<h3>{project.data.project.name}</h3>
						<p>{project.data.project.description}</p>
						{project.data.project.code && (
							<p>
								<a href={project.data.project.code}>GitHub</a>
							</p>
						)}
						{project.data.project.website && (
							<p>
								<a href={project.data.project.website}>
									{project.data.project.website}
								</a>
							</p>
						)}
					</article>
				))}
			</section>

			<section id="workflows">
				<h2>Work Flows</h2>
				<p>
					Noodlestan is built in the open, one small piece at a time,
					many larger xxx moving together.
				</p>
				<p>
					The projects live in separate repositories and are coordinated
					through a shared workspace. Each repository can be built and
					used on its own.
				</p>
				<p>
					Work is organised through roadmaps, milestones, plans, and
					iterations. Plans describe the work, its scope, commits, and
					how completion will be verified. Roadmaps and milestones
					coordinate priorities and dependencies.
				</p>
				<p>
					Projects also carry structured records (Art) describing what
					they are and how they relate to the rest of the ecosystem.
					These records are used as sources of truth by agents as well
					as by tools.
				</p>

				<h3>Artificial Work</h3>
				<p>
					AI agents are used extensively for planning, drafting, coding,
					documentation, and automating parts of the work. Since part of
					the work is to make everything artificial more effective I
					have been able to keep the bill under $20 bucks a month.
				</p>
				<p>
					The motivation of all this artificial work is to make the work
					more effective and to turn its results into reusable tools,
					conventions, and infrastructure. It operates under the
					principle that for every artificial process, there should be a
					plan to decompose it and extract every deterministic operation
					into something that can be made explicit, reproducible, and
					independent of the agent performing it.
				</p>
				<p>
					The AGENTS, skills, and other types of context files (a lot MD
					and a little Art here and there) are not public yet because
					the goal is to distribute them in different formats so they
					can be used in different platforms without the Noodlestan
					dependencies.
				</p>
				<p>
					The tools, conventions, and ways of working are themselves
					part of the experiment.
				</p>
			</section>

			<section id="contributing">
				<h2>Contributing</h2>
				<p>
					Did you find a bug? Do you have an idea? Want to contribute,
					discuss something, or just say hello?
				</p>
				<p>You are welcome.</p>
				<nav>
					<a href="https://discord.gg/4MBbPK7sU">Discord</a>
					<a href="https://github.com/noodlestan">GitHub</a>
					<p>
						Contributions, questions, bug reports, and ideas are
						welcome.
					</p>
					<a href="mailto:hello@noodlestan.org">Contact</a>
				</nav>
			</section>

			<section id="about">
				<h2>About</h2>
				<p>Hi, I am André Torgal.</p>
				<p>Noodlestan is a land of dreams, experimentation, and creativity.</p>
				<p>
					For the past few years I have been keeping my favourite
					projects and ideas here, without any real plan or particular
					goals. Writing software because. Just because.
				</p>
				<p>
					Meanwhile, I decided it was time to publish everything
					properly.
				</p>
				<p>
					So I have been working towards stable, documented releases of
					everything listed here, and making the whole thing easier to
					discover, use, understand, and contribute to.
				</p>
			</section>
		</main>

		<footer>
			<ul>
				<li>
					<a href="https://github.com/noodlestan">GitHub</a>
				</li>
				<li>
					<a href="https://discord.gg/4MBbPK7sU">Discord</a>
				</li>
				<li>
					<a href="mailto:hello@noodlestan.org">Contact</a>
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p>Copyright (c) 2026 Noodlestan https://noodlestan.org/</p>
		</footer>
	</body>
</html>
